{# Who is travelling, and in what. Shared by the home page, the results header
   and the rebook dialog, which carried three different sets of these -- four
   types on one, three on the others, different ids, different limits, and for a
   long time not one of them with a `name`, so no browser ever submitted a
   passenger count.

   Three types, matching what the URL spells and what the fare model prices. An
   infant here is on a lap: it takes no seat, which is why it is cheap, why it
   does not count against the seat limit, and why there may not be more of them
   than there are adults to hold them.

   One trigger, not four fields. Four controls will not fit a single-row bar --
   which is the whole reason the reference collapses them -- and "two adults in
   economy" is one decision anyway.

   The selects are still here, hidden. They are what submits, what the empty
   select disabler acts on so `children=&infants=` stays out of a shared URL,
   and what the page is left with if the script never runs. The steppers write
   to them; they are a layer on top, exactly as combo__native is for a place. #}
{% set prefix = prefix|default('passengers') %}
{% set panel_id = prefix ~ '-panel' %}
{% set types = [
    { 'key': 'adults', 'label': 'Adults', 'hint': '12+', 'from': 1, 'value': party.adults|default(1) },
    { 'key': 'children', 'label': 'Children', 'hint': '2–11', 'from': 0, 'value': party.children|default(0) },
    { 'key': 'infants', 'label': 'Infants', 'hint': 'on a lap, no seat', 'from': 0, 'value': party.infants|default(0) }
] %}
{% set cabin_value = cabin_value|default('economy') %}
{% set fare_shares = party_shares() %}
{# A map, so the label the trigger shows is a lookup rather than a loop: `set`
   inside a `for` does not escape it, and Twig 3 dropped the inline `for … if`. #}
{% set cabins = {
    'economy': 'Economy',
    'premium': 'Premium Economy',
    'business': 'Business',
    'first': 'First'
} %}
{% set cabin_label = cabins[cabin_value]|default('Economy') %}
{% set heads = types[0].value + types[1].value + types[2].value %}

<div class="party">
    <button type="button" class="party__trigger js-party-trigger"
            aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="{{ panel_id }}">
        <span class="searchbar__label">Passengers</span>
        {# Two facts, two elements. The hero sets them on their own lines the
           way the reference does -- it has no standing label, so these are what
           names the field -- and everywhere else they stay on one, under the
           label that is already there. #}
        <span class="party__summary js-party-summary"
              data-one="passenger" data-many="passengers">
            <span class="party__heads js-party-heads">{{ heads }} {{ heads == 1 ? 'passenger' : 'passengers' }}</span>
            <span class="party__class js-party-class">{{ cabin_label }}</span>
        </span>
    </button>

    {# The rules come from Party::fromCounts() as a number rather than being
       retyped in JavaScript: there is one definition of how many seats a booking
       may hold, and it is in PHP. #}
    {# The seat limit and the fare shares both come from Party as numbers rather
       than being retyped in JavaScript: there is one definition of how many
       seats a booking may hold and of what a child costs, and it is in PHP. The
       calendar reads these to price its days for the party without asking the
       server again. #}
    <div class="party__panel" id="{{ panel_id }}" data-max-seats="{{ max_seats }}"
         data-child-fare="{{ fare_shares.child_fare }}" data-infant-fare="{{ fare_shares.infant_fare }}"
         data-child-tax="{{ fare_shares.child_tax }}" data-infant-tax="{{ fare_shares.infant_tax }}" hidden>
        {% for type in types %}
            {% set id = prefix ~ '_' ~ type.key %}
            <div class="party__row">
                <span class="party__name" id="{{ id }}-label">
                    {{ type.label }}
                    <small>{{ type.hint }}</small>
                </span>

                <div class="party__stepper">
                    <button type="button" class="party__step js-party-less"
                            aria-label="Fewer {{ type.label|lower }}">&minus;</button>
                    <output class="party__value" for="{{ id }}" aria-live="polite">{{ type.value }}</output>
                    <button type="button" class="party__step js-party-more"
                            aria-label="More {{ type.label|lower }}">+</button>
                </div>

                <select class="party__native js-party-count" id="{{ id }}"
                        name="{{ config('search.form.input.' ~ type.key) }}"
                        data-floor="{{ type.from }}" aria-labelledby="{{ id }}-label"
                        tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
                    {# "No children" is the absence of a number, not a zero. #}
                    {% if type.from == 0 %}
                        <option value=""{{ type.value == 0 ? ' selected' : '' }}>0</option>
                    {% endif %}
                    {% for n in 1..max_seats %}
                        <option value="{{ n }}"{{ type.value == n ? ' selected' : '' }}>{{ n }}</option>
                    {% endfor %}
                </select>
            </div>
        {% endfor %}

        {# Radios rather than a select: four options, one choice, and no reason
           to hide three of them behind a click. Laid out two by two, because as
           full-width rows they made the panel taller than the dialog holding
           it. The input is the control; the tile is its label. #}
        <fieldset class="party__cabins">
            <legend class="party__name">Cabin</legend>
            <div class="party__cabin-grid">
                {% for value, label in cabins %}
                    <label class="party__cabin">
                        <input type="radio" class="js-party-cabin"
                               name="{{ config('search.form.input.class') }}" value="{{ value }}"
                               {{ cabin_value == value ? ' checked' : '' }} />
                        <span>{{ label }}</span>
                    </label>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>
        </fieldset>
    </div>
</div>
